{
  "preference": "B",
  "reason": "B better supports the director’s decision: its Design assessment treats amnesty as a possible explanation, whereas A’s Causal assessment asserts without evidence that amnesty affected both groups equally. B also explicitly proposes intention-to-treat analysis. Both correctly report identical 5-point increases and unchanged 20-point gaps, but A invents an evidential rationale for its 10-point threshold. B has a material tense error implying randomization already occurred, and both decision rules omit uncertainty.",
  "reports": {
    "A": {
      "task_fidelity": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Meets the note-length requirement, cites the supplied sources, calculates the relevant comparisons, and proposes a feasible randomized test and decision rule. Unsupported causal statements weaken the requested descriptive/causal distinction.",
        "excerpt": "Causal assessment: “because the amnesty affected both groups equally.”",
        "source_evidence": "S2 says only that the campaign “reached both groups,” not that its effects were equal."
      },
      "facts_and_evidence": {
        "score": 2,
        "reason": "The numerical comparisons are correct, but equal amnesty effects and a pilot-supported 10-point confounding magnitude are unsupported.",
        "excerpt": "Proposed next test: “I propose a 10-point difference ... since the pilot suggests plausible confounding effects of this size.”",
        "source_evidence": "S1 gives 20-point between-group gaps and 5-point within-group increases; neither source estimates a 10-point confounding effect. S2 states there is “no preset estimate of the smallest worthwhile benefit.”"
      },
      "explanation_and_synthesis": {
        "score": 2,
        "reason": "Recognizes that baseline imbalance undermines the raw pilot comparison, but overstates what explains the observed gap. An unchanged gap does not establish that reminders had no effect.",
        "excerpt": "Causal assessment: “it reflects pre-existing differences between people who opted in and those who declined, not the reminders' effect.”",
        "source_evidence": "S1 establishes equal observed gaps across periods. S2 reports voluntary enrollment and missing earlier trends, leaving the counterfactual change unidentified."
      },
      "counterevidence_and_boundaries": {
        "score": 2,
        "reason": "Identifies selection and missing trends, but turns a shared exposure into a known equal effect. It also omits uncertainty from the adoption rule.",
        "excerpt": "Causal assessment: “With voluntary enrollment, no prior-trend data, and no recorded opt-in reasons (S2), the pilot design cannot support a causal claim.”",
        "source_evidence": "S2 supports these limitations but establishes only shared campaign reach, not equal effects; it supplies no precision estimates for a future trial."
      },
      "reader_usefulness": {
        "score": 2,
        "reason": "Offers an implementable next test, but the arbitrary threshold is misleadingly justified as evidence-based. The binary rule could reject a worthwhile intervention after an inconclusive trial.",
        "excerpt": "Decision criterion: “Adopt reminders only if the randomized comparison shows at least the preset difference; otherwise decline.”",
        "source_evidence": "S2 permits randomizing offers and recording assignment, uptake, and returns, but requires management to set the worthwhile benefit; it provides neither a supported numerical threshold nor a trial sample size."
      }
    },
    "B": {
      "task_fidelity": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Meets the length and source constraints, supplies the relevant comparisons, and addresses design, next testing, and a decision criterion. Its assertion that randomization already occurred conflicts with the prospective brief.",
        "excerpt": "Proposed next test: “Because staff did randomize assignment, an intention-to-treat comparison ... would support a causal claim.”",
        "source_evidence": "S2 says staff “can randomize an offer of reminders next month”; the completed pilot was not randomized."
      },
      "facts_and_evidence": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Accurately reports all rates and differences and appropriately qualifies possible selection mechanisms and amnesty effects. The past-tense randomization claim is inaccurate.",
        "excerpt": "Descriptive results: “The gap between groups was 20 points in both the prior month and the pilot month”; Proposed next test: “staff did randomize assignment”.",
        "source_evidence": "S1 gives 75/100 versus 55/100 before and 80/100 versus 60/100 during the pilot. S2 explicitly says enrollment was “not randomized.”"
      },
      "explanation_and_synthesis": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Connects baseline imbalance, identical changes, and design limitations coherently. Intention-to-treat is appropriate, but the note should explicitly identify its estimand as the effect of offering reminders, not necessarily receiving them.",
        "excerpt": "Design assessment: “may explain the common 5-point rise”; Proposed next test: “an intention-to-treat comparison of on-time return rates between offered and not-offered borrowers”.",
        "source_evidence": "S2 documents shared amnesty exposure and permits randomizing offers while recording uptake, allowing assignment effects to be distinguished from receipt effects."
      },
      "counterevidence_and_boundaries": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Properly treats selection mechanisms and amnesty as possibilities and does not mistake the existing group gap for causal evidence. It does not address sampling uncertainty or an inconclusive next trial.",
        "excerpt": "Design assessment: “The 20-point group gap is descriptive, not causal evidence.”",
        "source_evidence": "S1 shows the same gap before treatment; S2 documents voluntary enrollment and unrecorded prior trends and opt-in reasons."
      },
      "reader_usefulness": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Provides a feasible assignment-based analysis and a clear prospective decision rule. The 5-point threshold is transparently a recommendation, but lacks a practical justification, and the expand/stop rule should account for uncertainty.",
        "excerpt": "Decision criterion: “management must define the smallest worthwhile benefit — I recommend 5 percentage points. If the randomized offer improves on-time returns by at least that margin, expand the program; otherwise, stop.”",
        "source_evidence": "S2 requires management to set a worthwhile benefit before examining results and confirms the feasibility of randomized offers. Neither source supplies costs or other grounds for choosing 5 points."
      }
    }
  },
  "condition_clues": "Both use similar sectioned memo formats and source labels. No reliable condition-identifying clues observed; formatting did not determine the judgment."
}
